WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.760
- The Royal Albert Hall
is one of the most famous

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.490
and prestigious concert halls
in Britain and the world.

00:00:05.490 --> 00:00:06.990
It's 150 years old,

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:11.730
and basically every world famous musician
and band has performed here at some point.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.290
The trouble is, the science of acoustics

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.900
wasn't really well developed
back in the 19th century.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.680
A tall, oval-shaped auditorium
with a domed glass roof,

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.240
well, it looked impressive and regal,

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:23.250
but it created an accidental echo chamber,

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:26.910
the exact opposite of the focused sound
that you'd want for a concert.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:29.460
Beautiful location, terrible sound.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.040
To see how they fixed it,
we need to go up,

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.680
to the parts that the
public aren't allowed into.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.840
- It was constructed without any thoughts

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:39.390
of acoustics, really,
but it was also glazed,

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:41.130
it was daylit, naturally lit.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.470
The Prince of Wales who
became King Edward VII

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.840
made a speech there at the public opening.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.580
Everyone heard his voice twice.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.760
So immediately they had to do something
to solve the acoustics,

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:53.250
and they installed what's
called a calico velarium,

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.950
it's like a sail cloth hung from the roof.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.750
It was partially successful,

00:00:57.750 --> 00:01:00.990
the inner-glazed roof
suffered from leakage,

00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:03.600
broken glass panes as a result
of the Second World War,

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.960
and also daylight really wasn't
suitable for a modern hall.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.870
The inner glazing of the dome was
removed and replaced in 1949

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:16.980
by an aluminium velarium,
which is what you see there now.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:21.450
In the late 1960s, the now-iconic
"mushrooms" were added to the hall.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:24.660
They are suspended, convex diffusers,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.300
and they help cut down
the echo tremendously.

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:31.830
In 2019, the sound system
was replaced completely

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.200
within the hall, and
the subjective response,

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.200
the reaction from audiences,
has been absolutely tremendous.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:39.180
- This is right at the top of the hall.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.530
That is the glass roof, it is
40 meters down to the floor,

00:01:43.530 --> 00:01:47.010
and just through there is an
air vent called the "corona",

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.160
and running across it
is a tension steel mesh

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:51.450
to let the technicians access it.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:55.170
I am assured by the team
here that it is fail-safe

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:56.693
and I can walk on it.

00:01:58.710 --> 00:01:59.610
Guy, hello!

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:03.210
- Hello, welcome to the corona.

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.117
- Oh, it's a lot cooler in here.

00:02:05.117 --> 00:02:08.305
[laughing] Must be...
oh, I looked down!

00:02:08.305 --> 00:02:11.280
[laughing] I should not have looked down.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.050
I mean, you're health and
safety officer here, right?

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:13.980
- That's right, yeah.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.560
- You're just casually
standing on the mesh?

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.650
- Mm-hm. Yep.
Feel free to step out with me.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.890
- That's a full fear
response from me, that.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:24.463
I was going to say, is there
anything I can grab onto?

00:02:24.463 --> 00:02:26.070
I was going to-
- That's very greasy.

00:02:26.070 --> 00:02:29.038
So most people hold my hand.

00:02:29.038 --> 00:02:32.400
Including the fire brigade!
- [laughing] I was going to ask,

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.110
like, who has to come out here?

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.540
Oh my god, it bounces. It bounces.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:37.860
Who has to come out here?

00:02:37.860 --> 00:02:43.373
- Technicians, people who need
to put shows on and rigging.

00:02:43.373 --> 00:02:46.350
- Haah, ha-haah. Putting all
my weight on that foot

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.575
is so, so difficult, bloody hell.

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:52.800
Oh my god, don't bounce!

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.930
- The weight of the
Royal Albert Hall roof,

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:59.910
the dead weight of the wrought-iron
structure is about 340 tonnes.

00:02:59.910 --> 00:03:03.128
The roof itself in total can take
about a thousand tonnes,

00:03:03.128 --> 00:03:07.740
inclusive of all permanent and imposed loads,
such as wind and snow loading.

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:10.410
There is approximately a
hundred motorized chain hoists

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.339
which hang from the original
wrought iron structure of the roof,

00:03:13.339 --> 00:03:15.356
which vary in their load rating,

00:03:15.356 --> 00:03:19.440
but some can carry up to 1-2 tonnes
of show infrastructure.

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.660
We have lighting rails,
which support lighting,

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:25.020
sound speakers, as well as
the mushrooms

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.050
which provide the acoustic
dampening of the auditorium.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.210
The mushrooms, despite their
quite substantial size,

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:32.250
only weight around 40kg.

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:35.670
All of the mushrooms,
show infrastructure, speakers,

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:39.780
is all hung from the original
wrought iron roof structure.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.920
- I am not sure I can move right now.

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:45.440
I'm genuinely...

00:03:47.610 --> 00:03:49.890
- Just look forward, just
look forward and walk.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:51.570
Just walk, there's no way you can fall.

00:03:51.570 --> 00:03:52.890
There's no way you can fall.

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.380
Just walk, look, just look at the pole,

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.180
and that's it, you've done it, well done.

00:03:57.180 --> 00:03:58.013
- Thing is-

00:03:58.013 --> 00:03:59.230
- Congratulations(!)
- Thank you.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:01.290
I know it's all my head

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:03.810
because the pole is
connected exactly the same

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:04.860
as this grid is.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.380
- So this is the corona, the crown,

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:10.143
and that is the oculus above us.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.260
- It's creaking.

00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:15.120
- That's the heat expanding,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:18.420
so temperatures are regularly
in the thirties and forties.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.400
- Typically, whenever there's
a fall-from-height risk,

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.590
the factor of safety on
the material properties

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:25.260
that would go into the design
of the tension wire grid

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.090
is 5:1, which means there's a

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:31.590
fivefold weight allowance of the
material property which it can support.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.956
- But the most surprising thing of all
about this roof

00:04:33.956 --> 00:04:35.760
is not how they fixed
the acoustics,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.580
it's not this terrifying
technicians' trampoline,

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.930
it's just out there,

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.630
and the best way to see
that is from the outside.

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:44.340
I'm going to need a hand, I just-

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:45.299
- Feel free.

00:04:45.299 --> 00:04:47.400
[Tom gasping]

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.100
- The roof was constructed
in full in Manchester

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:51.270
and then it was dismantled

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:55.170
and reassembled in London
through internal scaffold shoring

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:56.730
from the inside of the hall.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:59.190
Every beam and element of
the wrought iron trusses

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.860
was then winched up to the top of the roof

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.420
and then assembled in situ.

00:05:03.420 --> 00:05:06.060
The shoring was then removed once the roof

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:08.010
had been completed in full

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:11.070
and dropped by about 5/16ths of an inch

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:14.100
or about 8mm, which would've
been a heart stopping moment

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.690
I think for all those involved.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.030
- All of which leads to the
most starting thing for me,

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:20.030
the reason I made this
video, this roof,

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:23.730
this iron and glass dome with all that
equipment hanging from it,

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.310
it's not actually connected
to the rest of the building.

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:28.170
It's touching, sure,
it's resting just here,

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:30.270
you can see where the two structures meet,

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.210
but there's no cement, no
bolts, no attachments at all.

00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:35.880
This dome is just so incredibly heavy

00:05:35.880 --> 00:05:38.880
that it's not going to move,
and it hasn't for 150 years.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.930
600 tons of iron and glass,
and a load of Victorian engineering.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:44.190
It doesn't need anything more

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.320
because anything powerful
enough to shift this

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.300
wouldn't be stopped by a couple of bolts.

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.143
The roof of the Royal Albert Hall
isn't actually attached.

